{
 "_about": "The geomagnetic polarity time scale: which way the Earth’s field pointed when sea floor of a given age cooled, for the probe on the map (js/probe.js). Each chron: top (the younger end) and base (the older end) in millions of years ago, polarity n (normal, a compass points north) or r (reversed, it points south), the chron it belongs to, and a familiar name where there is one. Ages run from today to 121.4 million years; the M-sequence beyond (M0r to M44, to about 170 million years) waits on GTS2020 Table 5.4 being copied in. The floor’s ages (world-tiles/*-age.png) are on the GTS2012 timescale, this table on GTS2020; the two differ by less than a million years through most of the span, so near a boundary the needle can be a chron out.",
 "source": "Ogg, J. G. (2020), Geomagnetic Polarity Time Scale, chapter 5 of Gradstein, F. M., Ogg, J. G., Schmitz, M. D. and Ogg, G. M. (eds), Geologic Time Scale 2020, Elsevier; the boundary ages as tabulated for GTS2020 in PmagPy (Tauxe et al. 2016, Geochemistry, Geophysics, Geosystems 17, doi:10.1002/2016GC006307; pmagpy/pmag.py get_ts(\"gts20\"), BSD 3-clause), with the C34n span added from GTS2020’s Aptian base",
 "licence": "The ages are published facts (GTS2020); the tabulation is from PmagPy, BSD 3-clause; this file CC BY 4.0 like the rest of the stories folder",
